## 1️⃣ Error Handling

| Issue | Why it matters | Recommended change |
|-------|----------------|--------------------|
| **No global error‑handling middleware** | Uncaught exceptions or promise rejections will crash the process and may leak stack traces to the client. | ```js<br>// place after all route definitions<br>app.use((err, req, res, next) => {<br>  console.error(err); // log securely (use a logger like Winston)<br>  const status = err.status || 500;<br>  res.status(status).json({ error: 'Internal Server Error' });<br>});<br>``` |
| **No “404 – Not found” handler** | Requests to undefined routes currently hang or return the default Express HTML page, which can expose server details. | ```js<br>app.use((req, res, next) => {<br>  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Resource not found' });<br>});<br>``` |
| **Synchronous route handlers are safe now, but future async code needs `try/catch`** | An async `await` that throws will bypass Express’s error flow unless wrapped. | ```js<br>app.get('/users', async (req, res, next) => {<br>  try { /* async work */ } catch (err) { next(err); }<br>});<br>``` |
| **Process‑level error handling** | `uncaughtException` / `unhandledRejection` can leave the app in an inconsistent state. | ```js<br>process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason) => { console.error('Unhandled Rejection:', reason); /* graceful shutdown */ });<br>process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => { console.error('Uncaught Exception:', err); /* graceful shutdown */ });<br>``` |

---

## 2️⃣ Input Validation & Sanitisation

> The current routes take **no user‑supplied data**, but as soon as you add query‑string, body, or path parameters you’ll want a robust validation layer.

| Recommended library | Usage pattern |
|---------------------|---------------|
| **`express-validator`** (or `joi`) | ```js<br>const { query, validationResult } = require('express-validator');<br>app.get('/search', [<br>  query('q').trim().isLength({ min: 1, max: 100 }).escape()<br>], (req, res, next) => {<br>  const errors = validationResult(req);<br>  if (!errors.isEmpty()) { return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() }); }<br>  // safe to use req.query.q …<br>});<br>``` |
| **`helmet` sanitisation** | Helmet already sets security headers; it also helps mitigate some injection vectors. |
| **Limit request body size** | Prevents DoS via huge payloads. ```js<br>app.use(express.json({ limit: '1mb' }));<br>``` |
| **Rate‑limit sensitive endpoints** | Thwarts brute‑force or enumeration attacks. Use `express-rate-limit`. |

*Even if you don’t have any input today, scaffolding a validation middleware now will keep future code consistent and safe.*

---

## 3️⃣ General Security Best Practices

| Area | Recommendation | Example |
|------|----------------|---------|
| **HTTP security headers** | Use `helmet` (already mentioned) to set CSP, HSTS, X‑XSS‑Protection, etc. | ```js<br>const helmet = require('helmet');<br>app.use(helmet());<br>``` |
| **CORS** | Restrict origins to only those you need (or disable entirely if not required). | ```js<br>const cors = require('cors');<br>app.use(cors({ origin: ['https://my‑frontend.example.com'] }));<br>``` |
| **Logging** | Replace `console.log` with a structured logger (Winston, Pino) that can be configured for production vs. dev. | ```js<br>const logger = require('./logger'); // set up pino/winston<br>logger.info('Server running on port %d', port);\n``` |
| **Environment‑driven configuration** | Do not hard‑code the port; use `process.env.PORT`. Provide a fallback for dev. | ```js<br>const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;<br>app.listen(PORT, () => logger.info(`Server running on port ${PORT}`));<br>``` |
| **Graceful shutdown** | Capture `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT` to close the HTTP server and any DB connections. | ```js<br>const server = app.listen(PORT);\nprocess.on('SIGTERM', () => { server.close(() => { logger.info('Server stopped'); process.exit(0); }); });\n``` |
| **Dependency hygiene** | Run `npm audit` regularly; pin versions; consider `npm shrinkwrap` or `package-lock.json` for reproducibility. |
| **Static analysis** | Use tools like `eslint` (with the `security` plugin) and `npm run lint` as part of CI. |
| **Avoid information leakage** | Do not expose detailed error messages or stack traces to clients; keep them in logs only. |
| **HTTPS enforcement** | If the app is behind a reverse proxy (NGINX, Cloudflare), set `app.enable('trust proxy')` and redirect HTTP → HTTPS. | ```js<br>app.enable('trust proxy');<br>app.use((req, res, next) => { if (req.secure) return next(); res.redirect(`https://${req.headers.host}${req.url}`); });<br>``` |

