## 3. Proper Error Propagation

Right now the route handlers never throw, but if you later introduce async logic (database calls, external APIs) you need to handle rejections:

```js
app.get('/users', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const users = await getUsersFromDb(); // hypothetical async call
    res.json(users);
  } catch (err) {
    // Pass to the central error middleware
    next(err);
  }
});
```

If you prefer not to wrap every route, you can use a helper:

```js
const asyncWrap = fn => (req, res, next) => Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);
app.get('/users', asyncWrap(async (req, res) => {
  const users = await getUsersFromDb();
  res.json(users);
}));
```

